  "top_gaps": [
    {
      "id": "PRD-091",
      "severity": "important",
      "label": "Validate discovery with rubric and hard-fail integrity \u2014 Manual sampling leaves discovery quality and real-show integrity vulnerable to silent regressions, which would show up as AI outputs feeling off-brand or failing to resolve cleanly in the UI",
      "why": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "PRD-084",
      "severity": "important",
      "label": "Deliver surprising but defensible taste-aligned recommendations \u2014 Without a sharper acceptance contract, the discovery engine could drift toward safe-but-generic picks and miss the product\u2019s core promise of taste-aware surprise",
      "why": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "PRD-066",
      "severity": "important",
      "label": "Answer directly with confident, spoiler-safe recommendations \u2014 If Ask does not commit to an immediate answer shape, users may get pleasant but meandering responses before they get actionable guidance",
      "why": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "PRD-078",
      "severity": "important",
      "label": "Require concept selection and guide ingredient picking \u2014 Users can technically complete Explore Similar and Alchemy, but the missing guidance risks lower completion and weaker concept choices because the interaction is less self-explanatory",
      "why": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "PRD-028",
      "severity": "important",
      "label": "Use timestamps for sorting, sync, freshness \u2014 Timestamp fields exist, but without explicit sorting behaviors the app can feel inconsistent about what counts as \u201crecent\u201d and why two views order the same library differently",
      "why": ""
    }
  ],
  "narrative": {
    "overall_posture": "This is a strong implementation plan. It is architecturally concrete, execution-ordered, and unusually complete against the benchmark rider and the main product surfaces. The remaining weaknesses are not structural omissions; they are mostly places where the plan stops one layer short of turning product intent into crisp acceptance criteria.",
    "strength_clusters": "The plan is strongest in Benchmark Runtime & Isolation, Collection Data & Persistence, Show Detail & Relationship UX, and Settings & Export. Those areas have explicit schemas, route boundaries, state lifecycles, destructive-test strategy, and phased delivery slices that make the rider and the core collection behaviors implementable without guessing.",
    "weakness_clusters": "The partial items cluster around AI behavioral contracts rather than platform architecture. Ask, Concepts, Explore Similar, Alchemy, and discovery quality all have the right modules and flows, but a few of the product\u2019s sharpest qualitative requirements are described as intent, sampling, or validation helpers instead of being locked down as hard requirements.",
    "risk_assessment": "If the team executed this plan exactly as written, the first thing a stakeholder or QA reviewer would notice is not missing pages or broken persistence. They would notice uneven AI behavior: Ask may answer too indirectly, recommendation quality may become merely competent instead of delightfully taste-aware, and discovery regressions may slip through because the rubric is sampled rather than enforced.",
    "remediation_guidance": "The plan mostly needs tighter specification, not more architecture. The remaining work is to convert AI quality goals and a few nuanced UX rules into explicit acceptance criteria: where timestamps drive ordering, what Ask must do in its opening lines, what guidance the concept pickers must display, and what automated or blocking checks protect discovery quality before release."
  }
}
